1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Network Fault Observation asks one question: where does communication stop?

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Mac -> Internet -> AWS VPC -> EC2 -> Docker -> Go Server
Hypothesis -> Break -> Observe -> Explain -> Restore

3
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Baseline: all 5 of 5 observation points were healthy.

4
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Experiment 1 removed only the default route:
0.0.0.0/0 -> Internet Gateway

5
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:07,000
SYN: 5 reached EC2. SYN-ACK: 10 left EC2. Final ACK: 0.
The TCP handshake did not complete.

6
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Internal health: 22/22 HTTP 200. Docker: 22/22 Up.
The original hypothesis was partially wrong.

7
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:32,000
After restore: HTTP 200. Terraform: No changes.

8
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Kiro: Specs, Steering, Property-Based Testing, Power + AWS Docs MCP,
Custom Agent, AgentStop Hook, and Settings.

9
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A failed curl does not necessarily mean the application failed.
Observe each boundary separately.
